Appendix B — Detailed Proofs (Paper A)

B.1 Complete Proof of Theorem 1 (Necessity)

Statement (abridged). If an agent achieves return $J^\pi > R_{\text{abs}} + \varepsilon$ in an environment where constraint violation has absorbing failure dynamics, then $p_F \triangleq P(\tau \text{ enters } F) \to 0$ as $J^\pi \to J^*$, and consequently $\text{MCM}(\pi) \to 0$.

Proof.
We adopt the structural causal influence diagram (CID) formalism used by Richens & Everitt (2024) to define the decision task. Let $F \subset \mathcal{X}$ be the constraint‑violation set (states where at least one $h_i(x) < 0$). The environment is assumed to be absorbing: for any $x \in F$, $\sup_\pi V^\pi(x) \le R_{\text{abs}} < J^*$.

Decompose the value function along the sample path:

V^\pi(x_0) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\tau_F-1} \gamma^t r_t + \gamma^{\tau_F} V^\pi(x_{\tau_F}) \right]

where $\tau_F = \inf\{t : x_t \in F\}$ is the first hitting time of $F$. For any sample path, $V^\pi(x_{\tau_F}) \le R_{\text{abs}}$ by the absorbing assumption. Let $p_F = P(\tau_F < \infty)$. Since $\mathbb{E}[\gamma^{\tau_F}] \le p_F$, we obtain the bound:

V^\pi(x_0) \le \frac{R_{\max}}{1-\gamma} - p_F\left( \frac{R_{\max}}{1-\gamma} - R_{\text{abs}} \right)

If $V^\pi(x_0) > R_{\text{abs}} + \varepsilon$, solving for $p_F$ yields $p_F < 1 - \frac{R_{\text{abs}} + \varepsilon}{R_{\max}/(1-\gamma)} \to 0$ as $V^\pi(x_0) \to J^*$. Since each $\text{CV}_i$ is bounded on the feasible horizon, $\text{MCM}$ is dominated by a constant times $p_F$ and hence converges to zero.

Viability connection. The set $K$ of states satisfying all constraints is the state‑constraint set. The set of initial states from which infinite‑horizon constraint satisfaction is possible is the viability kernel $\text{Viab}(K)$, characterised by Aubin (1991, Theorem 3.3.4). A policy that keeps MCM zero everywhere simply selects controls from the regulation map of $\text{Viab}(K)$. Loss of viability — i.e., exiting $\text{Viab}(K)$ — bounds the expected return. This establishes the formal link between the existence of the viability kernel and the impossibility of simultaneously achieving high return and persistent constraint violation. □

B.2 Full Constructive Proof of Theorem 2 (Non‑Uniqueness)

Plant: 2‑link planar arm with configuration $q \in \mathbb{T}^2$, joint torques $\tau \in \mathbb{R}^2$, $|\tau_i| \le \tau_{\max}$. Dynamics $M(q)\ddot{q} + C(q,\dot{q})\dot{q} + g(q) = \tau$ with the standard Lagrangian form.

Controller 1 ($\pi_1$, joint‑space PD+): $\tau = K_p(q_d - q) + K_d(\dot{q}_d - \dot{q}) + \hat{g}(q)$. Latent state $z_1 = (q,\dot{q})$.

Controller 2 ($\pi_2$, Port‑Hamiltonian energy‑shaping): Let the end‑effector position be $x = f_{\text{kin}}(q)$, where $f_{\text{kin}}$ is the nonlinear forward kinematics derived from the product of exponentials formula (Murray et al., 1994). Define Hamiltonian $H = \frac{1}{2} p^T M_x^{-1}(x) p + V(x)$ with damping injection $D > 0$. The control law is $\tau = J^T(q)(-\partial V/\partial x - D \dot{x})$, which renders the closed‑loop system Port‑Hamiltonian with Dirac structure and guarantees passivity by construction (van der Schaft & Jeltsema, 2014). Latent state $z_2 = (x, p, H)$.

Non‑linear map obstruction: There exists no linear $W$ such that $W z_2 = z_1$ for all $q$ along the trajectory, because $x = f_{\text{kin}}(q)$ is nonlinear and the Jacobian $J(q) = \partial f_{\text{kin}}/\partial q$ is not globally constant. The relationship $q = f_{\text{kin}}^{-1}(x)$ is multi‑valued (inverse kinematics singularities). Hence the two internal representations are not globally linearly related, even though both achieve $\text{MCM} = 0$. □

---

Appendix C — Port‑Hamiltonian Model for the 2‑Link Arm (Paper A)

We follow van der Schaft & Jeltsema (2014).

The Dirac structure $\mathcal{D}$ of the system is defined on the bond space of flows and efforts. The Hamiltonian $H(q,p) = \frac{1}{2} p^T M^{-1}(q) p + V_g(q)$ (kinetic + potential energy). The dynamics are:

\begin{bmatrix} \dot{q} \\ \dot{p} \\ y \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & I & 0 \\ -I & -D(q) & G(q) \\ 0 & G^T(q) & 0 \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\partial H}{\partial q} \\ \frac{\partial H}{\partial p} \\ u \end{bmatrix}

Passivity proof: $\dot{H} = -\left(\frac{\partial H}{\partial p}\right)^T D(q) \frac{\partial H}{\partial p} + y^T u \le y^T u$ since $D(q) \ge 0$. $H$ serves as a Lyapunov function. This pH structure is the architectural invariant that guarantees $\text{CV}_{\text{energy}} = 0$ by construction.

---

Appendix D — Differentiable MCM Surrogate (Paper A & B)

Let $h(x) \ge 0$ be a scalar inequality constraint. We approximate $\max(0, -h)$ by the log‑barrier (softplus):

\phi_\alpha(h) = \frac{1}{\alpha} \log(1 + \exp(-\alpha h)), \quad \alpha > 0

Properties: $\phi_\alpha(h) \to \max(0,-h)$ as $\alpha \to \infty$; $\phi_\alpha$ is $C^\infty$ for all finite $\alpha$; $\phi_\alpha'(h) = -(1+\exp(\alpha h))^{-1}$.

The surrogate MCM for trajectory $\tau$ is:

\widetilde{\text{MCM}}(\tau) = \sum_{i \in \text{ineq}} \int_0^T \phi_\alpha(h_i(x_t)) \,dt + \sum_{i \in \text{eq}} \int_0^T \|g_i(x_t)\|^2 \,dt

The gradient with respect to policy parameters propagates through the differentiable physics model. Backpropagation uses the fact that $\phi_\alpha$ is smooth; this approach is analogous to soft‑constraint methods in PINNs and differentiable programming (Karniadakis et al., 2021).

---

Appendix E — Fiber Bundle Background (Paper A)

A principal fiber bundle $(E, B, \pi, G)$ consists of a total space $E$, a base manifold $B$, a projection $\pi: E \to B$, and a structure (Lie) group $G$ that acts freely on the fibers $\pi^{-1}(b) \cong G$. This formalism has been applied to robotic locomotion and perception — see Chirikjian (2012) and Dear (2018) for a comprehensive robotic treatment.

In our context, $B$ is the task space (physical states modulo gauge), and $G = \text{Diff}_0(F)$ is the connected component of diffeomorphisms of the internal feature space $F$. Two optimal agents $A_1, A_2$ with projectors $\pi_1, \pi_2$ are gauge equivalent if there exists $\varphi \in \text{Aut}(E)$ covering the identity on $B$ such that $\pi_1 = \pi_2 \circ \varphi$. This guarantees identical physical predictions while internal coordinates differ.

